<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Proto-kingdom of Nekhen</h1><p class="st-meta">3500 BCE – 3150 BCE (approx.) · capital Nekhen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The predynastic chiefdom of Hierakonpolis, home of the falcon god Horus and one of the cradles of Egyptian kingship.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekhen">Nekhen</a>, the Greek Hierakonpolis, lay on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> and was among the largest settlements of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqada">Naqada</a> period. By c. 3500 BCE it had a ceremonial precinct, breweries, pottery kilns and an elite cemetery, and its chiefs controlled trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and along desert routes to the south.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekhen">Nekhen</a> was the cult centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, the falcon god later embodied by every Egyptian king. Its Tomb 100, the 'Painted Tomb', bears the earliest known Egyptian wall painting, with boats, hunting scenes and a figure smiting captives. Excavations have revealed an oval ceremonial court and an elite cemetery with buried animals, including elephants, hippopotami and baboons, signs of an ambitious ruling ideology.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 3150 BCE its rulers seem to have merged with, or been overtaken by, the kings of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/proto-kingdom-of-thinis">Proto-kingdom of Thinis</a>. The town remained a royal religious centre: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmer-palette">Narmer Palette</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scorpion-macehead">Scorpion Macehead</a> were found in its temple's Main Deposit, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekhen">Nekhen</a> stayed the symbolic capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 3500 BCE – 3150 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:0.00%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>3500 BCE – 3150 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd>36th century BCE – 32nd century BCE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nekhen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Egyptian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Egyptian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hierakonpolis, Kingdom of Nekhen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-3325&amp;focus=proto-kingdom-of-nekhen" aria-label="Proto-kingdom of Nekhen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5549 1254 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Proto-kingdom of Nekhen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5869 1510L5842 1467"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5842 1467h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5869 1510h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5869 1510h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nekhen. <a href="/atlas?year=-3325&amp;focus=proto-kingdom-of-nekhen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
